<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Kingdom of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as New Kingdom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The New Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was the period of Egyptian history from about 1550 BCE to about 1070 BCE, covering the Eighteenth to Twentieth Dynasties. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a> expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>, Egypt became an imperial power, extending its rule into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and contending with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>. The decline of Egyptian control in Nubia later allowed the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt.jpg" alt="New Kingdom of Egypt" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArdadN, Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt_NK_edit.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Amarna age Ammishtamru I and Niqmaddu II corresponded with the pharaohs of the Egyptian Empire <b>New Kingdom</b>, but around 1350 BCE Niqmaddu II accepted the overlordship of the Hittite Empire, sealing a treaty with Suppiluliuma I that fixed an annual tribute in gold, silver and fine…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kushite Empire, also known as the Kingdom of Kush, arose in the Nile Valley south of Egypt, heir to the earlier Kerma culture that flourished from around 2500 BCE until its conquest by <b>New Kingdom Egypt</b> around 1500 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>New Kingdom</b> Senusret III was worshipped as a god in Nubia, and Greek writers knew Amenemhat III's mortuary temple at Hawara as the Labyrinth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Old Kingdom the King of Egypt, not called pharaoh until the <b>New Kingdom</b>, became a living god who ruled absolutely and could demand the services and wealth of his subjects.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>From the beginning of the <b>New Kingdom</b>, around 1550 BCE, the dead were buried with the Book of the Dead, or “Spells for Going Forth by Day”, a collection of nearly two hundred spells written on papyrus and often beautifully illustrated, such as the papyrus of the scribe…</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…about 2700 to 2000 BCE; Middle Egyptian, the classical language of the Middle Kingdom, used for inscriptions until the end of antiquity; Late Egyptian, the language of the <b>New Kingdom</b> from about 1350 BCE; Demotic, from the 7th century BCE; and Coptic, written from the 3rd century CE.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
